From fb1f12e85f2a821f89d1a9bf87895d239620c8b0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: abhay-byte <zenithblue.dev@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 07:48:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 09/10] kbase, panvk: opt-in evictable command buffer slabs
 (BASE_MEM_DONT_NEED)

Rationale: perf review 2026-10-26 part B, finding B4. All idle driver
memory is pinned: kbase's KBASE_IOCTL_MEM_FLAGS_CHANGE with
BASE_MEM_DONT_NEED puts a native region on the context's evict list,
whose shrinker frees the pages under memory pressure (the X6 is under
pressure all the time in Fallout 4), but nothing used it. B1's slab
caches free their idle slabs deterministically one window after they
were last needed; this lets the kernel decide instead.

kbase: kbase_kmod_bo_set_evictable(bo, evictable). Setting DONT_NEED
returns the ioctl's result. Clearing it re-backs what the shrinker took,
but kbase returns EINVAL for that on native regions (only imports get
past its flags update), so the result says nothing: the clear is followed
by a KBASE_IOCTL_MEM_COMMIT of every page, which kbase refuses for
DONT_NEED regions and which grows the region back if the re-backing fell
short. Only a successful commit makes the BO usable again. Imports and
growable BOs are refused. KBASE_IOCTL_MEM_FLAGS_CHANGE (nr 23) is added to
mali_kbase_ioctl.h.

panvk: with PANVK_KBASE_EVICTABLE=N, the slabs a cache window trim would
free are marked DONT_NEED and kept on the cache's evict list (outside the
cache lock: FLAGS_CHANGE takes the mm write lock), and freed N windows
later unless taken first. A take uses the evict list only when the cache
has no other slab, and frees the slab instead when the clear does not
verify. The first refusal to mark turns marking off for that cache with a
warning. Only slabs B1 has already found idle for a whole window are
marked, so the two ioctls per transition stay off the recording path
(with N = 0 nothing changes). Slabs in a cache belong to no command
buffer, nothing reads them from the CPU (the mode is off with pandecode),
and kbase frees DONT_NEED regions like others.

PANVK_DEBUG=mem: "evictable=<MiB>/<slabs> reused=<n> lost=<n>" after the
slab cache fields; lost counts slabs whose pages could not be brought back.

Knob: PANVK_KBASE_EVICTABLE (default 0 = off, 1-64 windows), read once at
device creation, kbase and PANVK_CMD_SLAB_CACHE only. Opt-in: the clear
semantics are from kbase sources and are untested on MTK kbase uAPI 1.21;
a PanProbe check (mark, memory pressure, take, CPU write, GPU read) should
come before anyone enables it. Arch: v10+ (CSF).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
